### **1\. Operational Role &amp; Organization**

* **Combined Arms Integration:** Attack helicopter units operate as companies or battalions[2]. They are typically employed as part of an **Air Cavalry Combat Brigade (ACCB)** or placed under **Operational Control (OPCON)** to a division, ground combat brigade, or Armored Cavalry Regiment (ACR)[2][3].
* **Terrain Domination:** While attack helicopters cannot secure or hold ground like infantry, they can **dominate terrain by fire** for limited periods[3][4].
* **Not Suited for Fortified Positions:** Attack helicopters are least effective against strongly fortified, dug-in defensive positions; they are best utilized against **moving enemy armor formations**[5].

---

### **2\. Survival &amp; Terrain Flying Modes**

Due to the range and lethality of modern Air Defense Artillery (ADA), attack helicopters must remain terrain-masked until engaging targets[6][7]. The manual defines three primary flight modes[7][8]:

1. **Low-Level Flight:** Constant altitude and airspeed along a preselected straight route; used over extended distances when enemy radar acquisition is unlikely[9].
2. **Contour Flight:** Constant airspeed with variable altitude conforming to the earth's contours; used forward of a division rear boundary[10].
3. **Nap-of-the-Earth (NOE) Flight:** Variable altitude and airspeed flying as close to the earth’s surface and vegetation as obstacles dictate; used when in close proximity to enemy forces[10].

---

### **3\. Movement Techniques &amp; Overwatch**

Movement across the battlefield is tailored to the **likelihood of enemy contact**[11]:

* **Traveling:** Used when contact is **not likely**; continuous movement at a constant airspeed[11][12].
* **Traveling Overwatch:** Used when contact is **possible**; lead elements move continuously while trail elements observe terrain and provide overwatch[11][13].
* **Bounding Overwatch:** Used when contact is **expected**; bounding elements move between covered positions while overwatching elements provide immediate suppressive fire coverage[11][14].

---

### **4\. Team Roles &amp; Target Handoff**

Attack helicopter teams rely on a division of labor between **Aeroscouts** and **Attack Helicopters**[15]:

* **Aeroscouts:** Proceed ahead of attack aircraft to see the battlefield, locate enemy formations, reconnoiter battle positions, hand off targets, and coordinate suppressive artillery or tactical air (TACAIR) support[16].
* **Attack Helicopters:** Move from a holding area to a battle position, partially unmask behind cover, acquire the handed-off target, unmask to fire, engage at maximum standoff range, remask, and move to alternate firing positions[20].

---

### **5\. Continuous Fire &amp; The One-Third Rule**

Because attack helicopters expend fuel (Class III) and ammunition (Class V) rapidly—requiring refueling and rearming **2 to 7 times per day**—units utilize the **One-Third Rule** to maintain continuous fire on station[23][24]:

* **1/3 on station** engaging the enemy[23][25].
* **1/3 en route** between the battle position and support area[23][25].
* **1/3 at the Forward Area Refuel and Rearm Point (FARRP)**[23][25].

*Note: The optimum distance between a battle position and the FARRP is* **17 to 25 km** *to keep it beyond normal enemy divisional artillery range while minimizing transit time*[24]*.*

---

### **6\. Tactical Employment in Offense &amp; Defense**

* **Offensive Operations:** Attack helicopters excel in **movement to contact, exploitation, and pursuit**[26][27]. They bypass enemy rear guards to strike withdrawing lead columns, command posts, communications nodes, and logistics complexes[27][28].
* **Defensive Operations:** Integrated into the **Covering Force Area (CFA)** and **Main Battle Area (MBA)** to blunt enemy armored penetrations, cover friendly ground maneuvers, and attack enemy flanks[29].